%[text] # Main Menu
%[text] Use these lessons in sequence. Begin with [Starting Tips](<matlab: edit StartingTips.m>) if you are new to MATLAB Live Scripts.
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"Table of Contents"}
%[text] ## Background
%[text] This courseware utilizes the [palmerpenguins](https://allisonhorst.github.io/palmerpenguins/articles/intro.html#installation) \[1\] dataset, which contains the measurements of the size of three different (and very cute!) penguin species that were found on three different islands in the Palmer Archipelago in Antarctica. These data were collected and [published](https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0090081) by Dr. Kristen Gorman between 2007 and 2009 as part of the Palmer Station Long Term Ecological Research Program \[2\] (linked pages have more information on the [data](https://github.com/allisonhorst/palmerpenguins#additional-data-use-information) and [licensing](https://creativecommons.org/share-your-work/public-domain/cc0/)).
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![](text:image:02ec)
%[text]{"align":"center"} Adelie penguins waddling around Snow Hill Island, [Antarctica](https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2007_Snow-Hill-Island_Luyten-De-Hauwere-Adelie-Penguin-16.jpg).
%[text] Biologists love to classify things, particularly organisms, into groups. We often do this based on the similarity of characteristics between organisms. You've probably seen the [internationally accepted system](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK21100/) for naming and classifying living things in a high school textbook. This system is based on a classification method introduced in 1758 by Carolus Linnaeus, commonly regarded as the "father" of modern taxonomy (what we name the branch of science concerned with classifying stuff). This classification method has many levels, but you only need two (genus and species) to uniquely identify an organism!
%[text] The [species](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Species) is the fundamental unit of taxonomy and a species is composed of a group of organisms that breed naturally in the wild and produce offspring that can *also* reproduce. A [genus](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Genus) is a group of closely related species. For example, a lion and a tiger are of the same genus but are different species (they do not naturally interbreed, though sometimes a 'liger' will be found in captivity as the result of breeding between the two. [Ligers](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Liger) either have reduced fertility or are infertile). In the case of our penguins, we have three species: *Adelie*, *Chinstrap*, and *Gentoo* penguins, all of which belong to the genus *Pygoscelis*.
%[text] These penguin species differ in size, appearance, foraging habits, where they live, and even how different males and females present within the species (sexual dimorphism). Looking at and quantifying the differences between species can give biologists a lot of really useful information. For example, does penguin size depend on the environment that they've evolved within? Do changes in body mass overall seem to predict other changes in that species (so if one penguin species tends to be bigger than another, is there a change in their bill size or shape)? Answering these  questions can tell us a lot about what factors drove the evolution of each species, or allow us to create computer programs that can classify penguins in the wild as one species or another.
